  <!-- DOCUMENT HEADER -->
  <div class="doc-header">
    <div class="doc-ref">POCZ.CC // FIELD MANUAL // REV 2026</div>
    <h1>Acer Chromebook C720 (Model ZHN) — UEFI &amp; Linux Conversion</h1>
    <p class="doc-summary">
      Procedure for removing hardware write-protection, flashing MrChromebox Full UEFI ROM, and converting an End-of-Life Acer C720 into a standalone Linux machine.
    </p>
    <div class="spec-grid">
      <div class="spec-item"><strong>Reg. Model</strong>Acer ZHN (C720 Series)</div>
      <div class="spec-item"><strong>Board Name</strong>PEPPY (Intel Haswell)</div>
      <div class="spec-item"><strong>Firmware Target</strong>MrChromebox Full ROM</div>
      <div class="spec-item"><strong>Internal Drive</strong>16GB M.2 2242 SATA</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- PREREQUISITES -->
  <div class="prereq-box">
    <h2>[00] Required Tools &amp; Media</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Phillips Screwdriver:</strong> Size <code class="inline">#0</code> or <code class="inline">#00</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>USB Flash Drive (8GB+):</strong> For the bootable Linux installer (plus an optional spare USB stick to back up your stock ChromeOS ROM).</li>
      <li><strong>Secondary Computer:</strong> To flash the Linux ISO onto your USB drive using <strong>BalenaEtcher</strong> or <strong>Rufus</strong>.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <!-- STEP 1 -->
  <div class="step-card" id="step-card-1">
    <div class="step-header" onclick="togglePoczStep(1)">
      <div class="step-title-wrap">
        <span class="step-num">01</span>
        <span class="step-title">Create Bootable Linux USB</span>
      </div>
      <label class="step-check-label" onclick="event.stopPropagation()">
        <input type="checkbox" class="step-check" onchange="togglePoczStep(1, this.checked)"> DONE
      </label>
    </div>
    <div class="step-sub">Requires a separate computer and an 8GB+ USB flash drive.</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <p>Download a 64-bit ISO for a lightweight Linux distribution tailored for 2GB–4GB of RAM and a 16GB SSD:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Linux Mint (XFCE Edition)</strong> or <strong>Xubuntu:</strong> Strong out-of-the-box hardware support and clean desktop layout.</li>
        <li><strong>Lubuntu</strong> or <strong>MX Linux:</strong> Ideal ultra-light footprint if your C720 is the 2GB RAM variant.</li>
      </ul>
      <p style="margin-top:0.5rem; margin-bottom:0;">Use <strong>BalenaEtcher</strong> or <strong>Rufus</strong> to write the <code class="inline">.iso</code> image to your USB drive.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- STEP 2 -->
  <div class="step-card" id="step-card-2">
    <div class="step-header" onclick="togglePoczStep(2)">
      <div class="step-title-wrap">
        <span class="step-num">02</span>
        <span class="step-title">Remove Hardware Write-Protect Screw</span>
      </div>
      <label class="step-check-label" onclick="event.stopPropagation()">
        <input type="checkbox" class="step-check" onchange="togglePoczStep(2, this.checked)"> DONE
      </label>
    </div>
    <div class="step-sub">Power off completely and unplug the AC adapter before opening the chassis.</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <ol>
        <li>Flip the C720 over and remove all <strong>13 screws</strong> from the bottom cover—there are 12 visible screws plus <strong>1 hidden screw underneath the white warranty sticker</strong> along the bottom-center edge.</li>
        <li>Gently pry off the bottom plastic cover starting near the rear hinge vents.</li>
        <li>Locate the <strong>Write-Protect (WP) screw</strong> on the left side of the motherboard between the Wi-Fi card and the keyboard ribbon cable. It is labeled <strong>7</strong> on the board with a <strong>large white arrow</strong> pointing at it, bridging two silver semicircular solder pads.</li>
        <li>Remove that screw completely (store it safely or tape it inside the plastic shell), snap the bottom cover back into place, and reinstall the outer case screws.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- STEP 3 -->
  <div class="step-card" id="step-card-3">
    <div class="step-header" onclick="togglePoczStep(3)">
      <div class="step-title-wrap">
        <span class="step-num">03</span>
        <span class="step-title">Enable ChromeOS Developer Mode</span>
      </div>
      <label class="step-check-label" onclick="event.stopPropagation()">
        <input type="checkbox" class="step-check" onchange="togglePoczStep(3, this.checked)"> DONE
      </label>
    </div>
    <div class="step-sub">Note: This wipes all local data currently stored on the Chromebook.</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <ol>
        <li>Plug the AC charger back in (removing the bottom cover trips a battery cutoff switch that requires AC power to reset).</li>
        <li>Hold down <kbd>Esc</kbd> + <kbd>Refresh (F3)</kbd> and tap the <kbd>Power</kbd> button once.</li>
        <li>At the yellow exclamation recovery screen, press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>D</kbd> (there is no on-screen prompt for this shortcut).</li>
        <li>Press <kbd>Enter</kbd> to confirm turning OS verification off.</li>
        <li>The machine will reboot and spend ~5 minutes transitioning to Developer Mode. When it returns to the white <em>"OS Verification is OFF"</em> screen, press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>D</kbd> to boot ChromeOS.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- STEP 4 -->
  <div class="step-card" id="step-card-4">
    <div class="step-header" onclick="togglePoczStep(4)">
      <div class="step-title-wrap">
        <span class="step-num">04</span>
        <span class="step-title">Open a Root Command Shell</span>
      </div>
      <label class="step-check-label" onclick="event.stopPropagation()">
        <input type="checkbox" class="step-check" onchange="togglePoczStep(4, this.checked)"> DONE
      </label>
    </div>
    <div class="step-sub">Connect to Wi-Fi on the welcome screen; do not sign in with a Google account.</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <p>Once connected to Wi-Fi on the initial setup screen, open a terminal using either method:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Method A (VT2 Console):</strong> Press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>Alt</kbd> + <kbd>F2 (Right Arrow →)</kbd>. At the login prompt, type <code class="inline">chronos</code> and press <kbd>Enter</kbd> (no password required).</li>
        <li><strong>Method B (Guest Crosh):</strong> Click <em>Browse as Guest</em>, press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>Alt</kbd> + <kbd>T</kbd> to open Crosh, type <code class="inline">shell</code>, and press <kbd>Enter</kbd>.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- STEP 5 -->
  <div class="step-card" id="step-card-5">
    <div class="step-header" onclick="togglePoczStep(5)">
      <div class="step-title-wrap">
        <span class="step-num">05</span>
        <span class="step-title">Flash MrChromebox UEFI Firmware</span>
      </div>
      <label class="step-check-label" onclick="event.stopPropagation()">
        <input type="checkbox" class="step-check" onchange="togglePoczStep(5, this.checked)"> DONE
      </label>
    </div>
    <div class="step-sub">Keep AC power connected throughout the flashing process.</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <p>Because the C720's legacy ChromeOS build has expired root SSL certificates, include the <code class="inline">-k</code> flag so <code class="inline">curl</code> bypasses certificate checks:</p>
      
      <div class="terminal-wrap">
        <pre class="terminal-cmd" id="pocz-fw-cmd">cd; curl -LOkf https://mrchromebox.tech/firmware-util.sh &amp;&amp; sudo bash firmware-util.sh -k</pre>
        <button type="button" class="copy-btn" onclick="copyPoczCmd(this)">Copy</button>
      </div>

      <ol>
        <li>Confirm the utility identifies <strong>Acer Chromebook C720 (PEPPY)</strong> and shows <strong>Fw WP: Disabled</strong>.</li>
        <li>Type <kbd>2</kbd> and press <kbd>Enter</kbd> for <strong>Install/Update UEFI (Full ROM) Firmware</strong>.</li>
        <li>Type <kbd>Y</kbd> to confirm. Insert a USB drive if prompted to save a backup copy of your stock BIOS.</li>
        <li>Wait for the ROM to download, write, and verify. Once complete, press <kbd>Enter</kbd> to return to the menu, then press <kbd>P</kbd> to power off.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- STEP 6 -->
  <div class="step-card" id="step-card-6">
    <div class="step-header" onclick="togglePoczStep(6)">
      <div class="step-title-wrap">
        <span class="step-num">06</span>
        <span class="step-title">Boot USB &amp; Install Linux</span>
      </div>
      <label class="step-check-label" onclick="event.stopPropagation()">
        <input type="checkbox" class="step-check" onchange="togglePoczStep(6, this.checked)"> DONE
      </label>
    </div>
    <div class="step-sub">Your C720 is now a standalone UEFI PC and will reclaim all 16GB of SSD space.</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <ol>
        <li>Insert your bootable Linux USB drive and press the <kbd>Power</kbd> button.</li>
        <li>Press <kbd>Esc</kbd> at the Coreboot splash screen to open the UEFI menu.</li>
        <li>Select <strong>Boot Manager</strong>, pick your <strong>USB Drive</strong>, and press <kbd>Enter</kbd>.</li>
        <li>Once the live desktop loads, launch the <strong>Install</strong> program, select the <strong>Chromebook</strong> keyboard layout when prompted, choose <strong>Erase disk and install</strong>, and finish setup.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- FIELD NOTES -->
  <div class="notes-box">
    <h2>[APPENDIX] Hardware &amp; Post-Install Notes</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Top-Row Function Keys:</strong> Selecting the <em>Chromebook</em> keyboard layout during Linux installation maps the top-row media, volume, and brightness keys natively.</li>
      <li><strong>Storage Upgrade (M.2 2242 SATA):</strong> Unlike later Chromebooks with soldered eMMC chips, the C720 uses a socketed <strong>42mm M.2 SATA (NGFF)</strong> drive next to the battery. You can drop in a 64GB or 128GB M.2 2242 SATA SSD (not NVMe) while the bottom cover is off.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <script>
    function copyPoczCmd(btn) {
      var cmdText = document.getElementById('pocz-fw-cmd').innerText;
      navigator.clipboard.writeText(cmdText).then(function() {
        var original = btn.innerText;
        btn.innerText = 'Copied!';
        setTimeout(function() {
          btn.innerText = original;
        }, 2000);
      });
    }

    function togglePoczStep(stepNum, explicitState) {
      var card = document.getElementById('step-card-' + stepNum);
      var checkbox = card.querySelector('.step-check');
      var isChecked = (typeof explicitState === 'boolean') ? explicitState : !checkbox.checked;
      checkbox.checked = isChecked;
      if (isChecked) {
        card.classList.add('completed');
      } else {
        card.classList.remove('completed');
      }
    }
  </script>
